            <ArticleTitle>Bilateral temporomandibular joint pseudogout.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>25-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pseudogout is an acute presentation of one type of crystal deposition disease in which calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals are found in the joint spaces of synovial joints. In this case, a 56-year-old caucasian male presented with right sided preauricular swelling, temporomandibular joint arthralgia and restricted mouth opening; he developed identical symptoms on the left side two days later.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
